## 7. Why Drive Family and Firmware Generation Matter

A manufacturer name alone is not enough to select a procedure.

Support and behaviour can differ between drive families, generations, firmware revisions, capacities, board revisions, and internal configurations.

Even two drives that appear nearly identical may not support the same operations or use the same internal structures.

Before selecting a procedure, record as much of the following as can be obtained safely:

- Manufacturer and full model number.
- Reported firmware revision.
- Capacity and interface.
- Relevant board identifiers, where applicable.
- Symptoms and when they began.
- Whether identification is consistent.
- Whether the drive disconnects, resets, or behaves abnormally.
- Any previous repair or recovery attempts.
- The exact tool name and version.
- The operation being considered and its documented support status.

Do not assume that a tool's support for identification also means that it supports every advanced function for that drive.

Likewise, a procedure that worked on one model should not be copied to another simply because the labels look similar.

### Compatibility is more than a model number

Compatibility can depend on the interaction between hardware, firmware, unique configuration information, and the tool's implementation.

The safest approach is to verify the exact combination rather than infer compatibility from superficial similarities.

